From 5a528cc90cc8117e54c13f3b437df2bd36307d97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:54:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] aux: never close a per-call client under its
 abandoned provider daemon's TLS read

A hard cancel of a protected aux call (a user Stop, the core-update pause's
compression yield) lets the owner unwind at once while the
hermes-protected-aux-provider daemon keeps reading the client's TLS stream.
Class routing builds one client per call and closed it in the owner's
finally: httpx close()d the socket the daemon was blocked on. The SSL
object keeps the raw fd in its BIO; the kernel gave the number to the next
open() (a SQLite database), and the daemon's next SSL_read took "SQLit"
from the header and wrote a 24-byte TLS 1.3 alert at offset 5: hub
inbox/inbox.db 2026-11-13 09:01:27 (3 ms after "compression yielded to a
Hermes update pause") and user-steps/actions.sqlite3 2026-11-11.

- agent/aux_provider_handoff.py: each protected call has a ProviderAttempt;
  on cancel the owner records it in the enclosing client_scope. The scope's
  release then only shuts the client's sockets down (FD-safe from any
  thread, wakes the daemon and stops billing the abandoned summary) and the
  daemon closes the client when its callback returns. No abandoned daemon:
  closed on the caller as before.
- class_routed_sync wraps each attempt in client_scope.
- agent/ssl_close_tripwire.py: HERMES_DEBUG_SSL_CLOSE=1 (installed from
  setup_logging) logs the stack when an SSLSocket is closed while another
  thread is inside recv on it.

Test: a real loopback TLS 1.3 server streams to the protected daemon; the
owner is cancelled, the socket's fd number is handed to a SQLite file when
released, and data wakes the daemon. Before the fix the header reads
b'SQLit\x17\x03\x03\x00\x13...' (the incident bytes); after it the header is
intact and the daemon thread closes the client.

Card: t_01a1d4495a984fc9
